246
00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:17.360
So we tried to avoid that and to sort of convince something that's worth doing

247
00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:21.120
and then go over the diff and merge the PR

248
00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:25.040
with a human review, and that sort of closes the loop.

249
00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:27.040
And then it

250
00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:30.040
didn't work right, because that's how it is.

251
00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:33.680
First of all, some of these offers were plausible

252
00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:37.680
but unverified, which is kind of like what we do when we go over the code.

253
00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:39.920
It's like, well, we can probably optimize this,

254
00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.240
but we don't know if that's really the bottleneck.

255
00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:45.280
If something is taking 20s or five

256
00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:48.320
seconds, I'm not sure exactly where that time is spent.

257
00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:51.240
So things that sound right are not necessarily

258
00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:53.160
the ones that are going to move the needle.

259
00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:55.680
Second was specifically for queries.

260
00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:58.720
So queries can be very complex.

261
00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:02.680
And also they are very dependent on how the code actually runs

262
00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:03.720
and where the data is.

263
00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:08.160
If you have that customer with like 10,000 rows returning

264
00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:11.160
from the database, it's not a big surprise that it's going to take longer.

265
00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.920
It's just that you have no idea how that looks like.

266
00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:19.360
And of course, the biggest, biggest problem is what we call the lazy fix,

267
00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.080
which means, oh, there's an exception, let's catch it.

268
00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:27.000
This is great, but it's not helpful at all.

269
00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:31.880
And a lot of time, what happens is that if you try to optimize

270
00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.880
for the syntax error or the long running query,

271
00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:37.920
then the question and the answer will be local.

272
00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:41.560
And we actually want to look at it from a broader perspective.

273
00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:47.440
So those are like things that I guess it's fine that they will start that way.

274
00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.320
But in time, we understood that.

275
00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:52.080
We want to explain what a fix does look like

276
00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:55.400
and what what am I interested in, kind of like a staff engineer

277
00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.840
would explain to someone who just joined the team.

278
00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.600
And all you need is context, right?

279
00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:05.480
And there are only two problems with context.

280
00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:09.800
One is you might have too much of it, and then it's really hard to understand

281
00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:10.480
what matters.

282
00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:15.000
And the other is you might not have enough and then it won't matter

283
00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:19.320
because the agent would just assume what's going on there.

284
00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:24.640
Or what I would do as an engineer is I would probably add more logs or metrics,

285
00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.680
deploy a version, gather context, understand

286
00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:31.160
where the bottleneck is, and then go and fix it.

287
00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:34.200
What the agent will do is to say, okay, this is what I have.

288
00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:35.720
Let's find the shortest path.

289
00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:39.480
Let's let's assume, take some assumptions and see where that goes.

290
00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:44.200
We can't do that to scale, especially if we want to interrupt engineers flow.

291
00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:45.720
Right. That's like the most important.

292
00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:46.440
We love that.

293
00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:51.600
So if I am going to flag an opportunity, I can't risk

294
00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:54.720
just saying something that won't really make sense

295
00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:58.240
because then they will read my report, they will open a pull request,

296
00:15:58.240 --> 00:15:59.960
they will review it and then deploy it

297
00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:02.120
and then find out that it didn't change anything.

298
00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:05.120
And then I just wasted their time.

299
00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.720
So it was really important for us to make sure

300
00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:11.000
that we have just enough context so that we can understand

301
00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.800
and have confidence in the fact that it's actually going to do something.

302
00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:17.880
But that's actually hard to do because they don't speak the same language.

303
00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:21.040
You know, the production context is usually in service level

304
00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:22.000
or endpoint level.

305
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:26.960
This endpoint takes five seconds and the P99 is six

306
00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:30.960
six seconds, and the P 100 is 17 seconds.

307
00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:34.520
And then the agent the agent reasons overcoat right.

308
00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:37.400
The local functions and the files and the class methods.

309
00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:41.320
It can kind of deduct the connection between the two,

310
00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:44.560
but they don't speak the same language.

311
00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:49.760
And that's where most of the of the problem is when you ask

312
00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:50.800
why is it long.

313
00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:53.360
It could go over the code and it might get it right.

314
00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:57.160
Don't get me wrong, some of the issues can be found in static

315
00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:00.400
analysis, but when we want to make that transition between

316
00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:04.760
human lead to an agent workflow, we have to be quite certain

317
00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:07.840
in our confidence so that we can truly automate it.

318
00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:09.720
If something works 90% of the time.

319
00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:13.160
It's not an automation, it's streamlining humans.

320
00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:16.480
And that's

321
00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:20.280
why we built what we call prod to code, which is basically a mapping

322
00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:23.640
of what's going on in production to the function level.

323
00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.840
So you have like the endpoint or the service

324
00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:30.640
and the endpoints or event consumers and the job cron jobs that it runs,

325
00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:34.520
and then the mapping of the functions that are involved within it.

326
00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:40.000
So one thing that we got from this is the ability to ask this is slow why.

327
00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.040
But it also opened a huge window

328
00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:47.080
of opportunity to ask the inverse question of I'm going to touch this.

329
00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:48.920
What does that impact?

330
00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:50.720
And should I care about it?

331
00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:54.440
Is it going to touch my payments and my authorization or not?

332
00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.880
So basically

333
00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:02.000
what the sensor is doing in

334
00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:06.080
that aspect is complete function level context in a way

335
00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:09.960
that's connected to the endpoint and deep forensic context only when it needed.

336
00:18:09.960 --> 00:18:13.200
And the assumption here is that we can't just go through like

337
00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:17.000
gigabytes of logs and spans and traces to be able to do that.

338
00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:21.680
But if we have for each of these functions, the ability to connect them to

339
00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:27.000
how often it runs, which business flows, does it impact, how long it takes,

340
00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:31.520
and whether it's failing, then we can start going over

341
00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:34.520
the deeper context, whether that's logs or traces or whatever,

342
00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:39.600
when we kind of have the area of where we are trying to to tap into,

343
00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:45.440
and that's the same level at which the coding agents reason.

344
00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:47.760
So we have the

345
00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:51.760
basic query language layer we specifically use ClickHouse for that.

346
00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:55.000
But of course every team has their own.

347
00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.840
And on top of that we can build a set of skills

348
00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:00.920
because it's not just enough to connect the skills

349
00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:04.800
to connect the data and kind of ask the agent to see what happens there.

350
00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:08.440
I saw like five different talks about why that fails.

351
00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:13.720
So we have the skills of how to approach an HTTP 500,

352
00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:18.200
how to approach a memory leak, how to approach a performance degradation.

353
00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:22.800
And all of a sudden it's not just Dave that can tap into that knowledge

354
00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:24.120
and expand it.

355
00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:27.760
And on top of that, or the level of the automations.

356
00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:32.640
So auto fix or dead code removal, a building on top of those skills

357
00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:34.480
that are building on top of the query language.

358
00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:38.280
And I think it was really important in terms of what we learned to allow

359
00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:39.600
all of these layers.

360
00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:41.560
The coding agent might need something

361
00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:45.480
that I cannot express in words, and that's why tools are not enough.

362
00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:49.720
But at the same time, I don't want to waste my tokens

363
00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:53.520
and my chain of thought on things that I know.

364
00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:56.640
I do know how to tackle a memory leak.

365
00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:59.560
I want to look at the pod that's had that memory leak.

366
00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:01.800
I want to look at a different one that didn't,

367
00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:03.360
and I want to see what happened there.

368
00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:04.800
That didn't happen there. Right?

369
00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:07.240
I when I look at the performance degradation,

370
00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:09.560
I want to understand where the time is being spent.

371
00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:14.240
So that small level of involvement

372
00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:17.920
and ownership on the methodology was very impactful for us.

373
00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:21.800
And then we can say something like, look for artificial

374
00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:24.800
delays like sleeps and timeouts,

375
00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:28.640
look for n plus one queries, look for missing indexes or synchronous

376
00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:31.800
blocking or sequential awaits all of these things.

377
00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.800
And when you think of a code base that exists in their case

378
00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:38.160
for 15 years, obviously these things are there.

379
00:20:38.200 --> 00:20:42.280
It's just that we need to kind of dig them out and know that they exist.

380
00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:43.400
And then you have these moments

381
00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:47.320
we're like, oh my God, this was like this for six years.

382
00:20:47.360 --> 00:20:48.760
Are you serious?

383
00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:52.720
But if we look at it from a positive perspective, there's like

384
00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:54.080
so much we can do.

385
00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:58.320
And I think in that aspect that gave us that

386
00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:01.400
balance between

387
00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:04.880
kind of letting it run free and having that full,

388
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:09.640
full freedom, but also kind of guiding it to how we know

389
00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:12.800
that engineering practices and skills work.

390
00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:17.520
And then we can ask something like, why are my endpoints taking so long,

391
00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:22.400
like my product manager asks, and get an answer of, oh, there's actually this thing

392
00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:26.400
with an N plus one query that exists in the code base for like six years.

393
00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:28.280
Maybe we can do something about this.

394
00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:32.360
And we were very happy and pleased about the results.

395
00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:33.640
So we said, okay, maybe

396
00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:38.440
we can just like add open pull requests for all of these high impact, low

397
00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:42.320
risk changes that don't require migrations or anything super scary.

398
00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:44.600
And we can just open a pull request.

399
00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:49.560
No no no no no,

400
00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:53.040
we don't want any pull requests that no one's going to go over

401
00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:57.480
and we don't want to go over those 80 progress even though they exist.

402
00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:00.800
And I think that's like if I if you can take one thing out of

403
00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.960
this is like after you get it working and you want to automate it,

404
00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:08.600
think about the scale and think about the humans that are still in the loop.

405
00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:11.360
We're not in the point where no one just cares about it.

406
00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:14.880
And if you open a pull requests, it's kind of like opening your Datadog

407
00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:19.240
and Sentry with those like 700 issues that all they say is like, well,

408
00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:23.920
I'm not going to be able to fix this, so I'm not even going to try.

409
00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:27.520
So we of course, we started with automated pull requests.

410
00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:29.920
We realized no one cares about that.

411
00:22:29.920 --> 00:22:34.280
And if it's not prioritized, then they're not going to do something about it.

412
00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.480
And again, I am not complaining.

413
00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:38.640
I'm not judging.

414
00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:39.720
We're builders, right?

415
00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:42.720
We have prioritization and it exists for a reason.

416
00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:47.360
No one has time to just go over a bunch of four requests built by the agent

417
00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:51.680
that are statistically huge, and try to understand what happens.

418
00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:52.800
We don't want to own that.

419
00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:54.920
We don't want to go into it.

420
00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:59.920
So we actually need to convince the human that it's worth the attention.

421
00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:01.760
Instead of convincing the agent

422
00:23:01.760 --> 00:23:04.840
that it's worth the tokens, he will always think that they are.

423
00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:08.280
So we map the hot paths that endpoints

424
00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:11.320
that are invoked often the business impact of that.

425
00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:15.280
So is this impacting payments or authentication

426
00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:18.640
or the set of things that you already know that you care about for your business?

427
00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:21.320
And also the risk?

428
00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:24.960
We are not looking for the best optimizations.

429
00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:30.720
We are looking for the highest impact, lowest risk changes that can be done

430
00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:34.600
so that we can go to the developer or the PM and say, hey, listen,

431
00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:38.880
it's like that small and it's going to do like 30% improvement.

432
00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:44.680
So we should do that and we want to make that humanly readable.

433
00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:49.480
So if a human gets something like hey, this endpoint is like

434
00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:54.000
the P90 is around 100 milliseconds, except it kind of takes 45 seconds

435
00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:57.760
every once in a while, and we have no idea why, theoretically speaking.

436
00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:00.560
And then it would be, oh, this is happening

437
00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:04.480
because you always use the Mongols distinct function.

438
00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:09.520
If you switch it to a search, then that should slow you down

439
00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:12.640
like improve like 30, 40%.

440
00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:15.680
And this is something that we can actually prioritize.

441
00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:19.640
And once we started talking about these quick wins,

442
00:24:19.800 --> 00:24:24.080
you can see that they can either open it to to dive deeper,

443
00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:26.520
they can create a ticket or create a PR because again,

444
00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:29.920
I don't want to be opinionated on exactly how advanced they are.

445
00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:31.320
That's fine.

446
00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.200
Either of those work, we can do it later.

447
00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:34.280
We can do it now.

448
00:24:34.280 --> 00:24:37.280
Obviously, both are labeled so that we can measure it.

449
00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:41.520
And all of a sudden that endpoint that used to take 100 milliseconds,

450
00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:45.080
except the fact that it kind of takes 45 seconds every once in a while.

451
00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.440
Well, it doesn't anymore.

452
00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:48.040
And I think that was a very,

453
00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:51.880
very big learning for us that we just want to get to a point where

454
00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:56.120
the humans kind of make the decision that it's worth a while.

455
00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:59.240
The fact that merging to production did not become it's

456
00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:03.320
not free, it's just cheaper to to merge and to ship.

457
00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:08.000
And then we kind of have the ability to learn more about it.

458
00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:11.960
So for things you can take from our experience

459
00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:16.200
so far, one, we still need to define what matters.

460
00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:19.520
And I think if I can be a bit philosophical, even though

461
00:25:19.520 --> 00:25:23.160
it's a use case, I think that's going to be a huge part

462
00:25:23.280 --> 00:25:27.600
of what we do as engineers in the future is decide what's worth a while,

463
00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:28.520
worth the tokens,

464
00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:32.320
worth the time, whether it's a human that's reviewing it or an agent.

465
00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:36.920
We still define what matters more and what matters less than the ability

466
00:25:36.920 --> 00:25:41.880
to score by what will be impactful for the business is really important.

467
00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:45.240
And I think that's that's something that we can all take to our day to day lives.

468
00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:49.680
And if we inform our agents on how we look at what they do,

469
00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:53.640
then they're going to do better because they don't just lack the runtime context.

470
00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:56.480
They also lack the business context.

471
00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:59.800
Second part is to automate the investigation phase to

472
00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:01.320
make prioritization better.

473
00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:04.080
And this is an example that we had for performance.

474
00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:07.720
We're doing similar things for reliability and the other things.

475
00:26:07.720 --> 00:26:10.560
But I'm not saying that the backlog should be empty

476
00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:13.040
just because we have agents.

477
00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:14.440
I'm saying that we have a way

478
00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:18.200
to use the agents to make better decisions on what to prioritize.

479
00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:23.320
And this is a use case that is not as common as what we're seeing.

480
00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:28.640
Like a lot of people are using agents to do what they were planned to do faster,

481
00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:32.440
and this was a way for us to help people

482
00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:37.880
prioritize the the ROI and not just the task itself.

483
00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:41.720
Context over cleverness, I guess.

484
00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:46.160
Like it's it's it seems obvious, but the agents get useful

485
00:26:46.160 --> 00:26:47.720
once they see what's going on.

486
00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:53.600
In our case, it was production context, being able to understand where it is

487
00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:57.280
and to have very high signal data about it

488
00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:02.560
so that they can build it the same way that a staff engineer

489
00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:06.240
cannot just move to a different company and ship to production on day one.

490
00:27:06.280 --> 00:27:07.840
That's the context that matters,

491
00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:11.720
and it's going to change their behavior the same way that it changes our behavior.

492
00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:15.680
And last but not least, Agentic engineering

493
00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:18.200
is not like coding with an agent.

494
00:27:18.200 --> 00:27:20.040
It is different.

495
00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:24.400
If we want something to work as a workflow, it has to actually work

496
00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:26.800
and it has to be confident in what it does.

497
00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:29.520
It is better to do less but the right things.

498
00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:34.400
And if I at any time I would suggest something that won't work.

499
00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:38.520
We lose trust in the process, so the automation unlocks

500
00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:42.680
continuous impact, but it also requires a higher confidence level.

501
00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.880
So choose the things that you feel

502
00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:48.440
that your repo and your automation level are ripe for.

503
00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:50.440
But I think that's where we're going.

504
00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:52.880
And when we try to think about that, our core engineers,

505
00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:56.040
when we know the scale, we can architect the system.

506
00:27:56.040 --> 00:27:59.040
So I think the scale that we're going to see,

507
00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:03.320
and the question that I'm asking myself is, how would would it look like

508
00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:07.440
to get to a point where some engineers and some of our customers

509
00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:11.680
companies would actually just click that merge button and get that fixed as is?

510
00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:14.120
And because I can measure it now,

511
00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:16.800
we can get better and better until we get to that point.

512
00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:18.040
And I think, you know,

513
00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:22.480
we can talk a lot about how that would be perfect if it just works.

514
00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:26.800
But the thing that was, I think really int about the process

515
00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:31.440
here is the ability to start with something that is still net positive,

516
00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:34.960
and it allows you that flywheel of understanding where it got stuck,

517
00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:37.440
where the insights not good enough. Were they good enough.

518
00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:39.440
But it was hard to merge them.

519
00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:40.240
Did we merge them.

520
00:28:40.240 --> 00:28:42.880
But they they didn't give that effect that we tried.

521
00:28:42.880 --> 00:28:45.720
And that let's us learn and improve.

522
00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:47.880
Before we get to that point of full automation.

523
00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:51.880
And again, my dream is to get to a point where people would say, well,

524
00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:54.280
80% of the time we just did the exact same thing

525
00:28:54.280 --> 00:28:58.120
and we kind of merged it to main, and then we will know that

526
00:28:58.160 --> 00:29:01.880
we're ready, but we're providing value along the way as we're getting there.

527
00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:05.000
When the models are getting better, the context is getting better,

528
00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:09.360
and everyone can kind of spend their time on on the right things.

529
00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:11.200
So thank you.

530
00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.040
I hope that was somewhat helpful.

531
00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:23.960
I appreciate your attention

532
00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:26.960
in this tough hour and we have some time for questions.

533
00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:30.160
Right. We have one minute, one questions.

534
00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:33.160
If someone has a very quick question

535
00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:36.920
anyone?

536
00:29:37.120 --> 00:29:40.120
One in the back.

537
00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:43.480
Yeah.

538
00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:46.240
If someone has a question that requires more than a minute,

539
00:29:46.240 --> 00:29:49.320
I'll stay outside to to answer them.

540
00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:51.200
Thanks a lot for the talk.

541
00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:52.360
I have one question.

542
00:29:52.360 --> 00:29:55.440
So most people we probably already have integrated with, like Datadog

543
00:29:55.440 --> 00:30:00.880
or Sentry or some other, like platform where you need

544
00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:04.920
to ingest loads of data to be able to get all these production metrics.

545
00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:06.800
So how do you stay competitive with it?

546
00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:08.040
Because, yeah, I guess you need to

547
00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:11.760
also ingest that amount of data, but then people won't be like, oh, well,

548
00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:16.480
we just like also pay you 10-K a month or whatever to be able to do this stuff.

549
00:30:16.480 --> 00:30:18.440
So to how do you figure that out?

550
00:30:18.440 --> 00:30:22.080
That's a huge question and especially for a startup.

551
00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:25.000
So we always like to say that we're like an espresso shop.

552
00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.680
We only have a spesso, but it's the best one in town.

553
00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:29.600
We try to focus on the thing that we do better.

554
00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:33.760
If they have Datadog or all these tools, we will ask, okay, but

555
00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:37.720
do you still suffer from production incidents and issues?

556
00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:39.440
Do you still spend way too much time?

557
00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.200
Are you still surprised by issues that your customers report?

558
00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:46.640
And if so, let's run together, obviously, like for example, distributed tracing.

559
00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:49.440
If they have that, we will just connect to what they already have.

560
00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:53.360
The thing is the function level context and the forensic context

561
00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:55.200
that is being proactively captured.

562
00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:59.000
And we focus on that and integrate to whatever it is that they have.

563
00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:02.080
Okay.

